John H. wrote:
> 
> Acid -can- be cancelled into water, if memory serves, but I think
> that's either a bug or a clever way of thinking about chemistry.  The
> definition of an acid makes it inherently chemical, so I'd say that
> some chemical are essential.
> 
> That said, chemistry is not my strong suit.  Is it true that
> acid+salt=base?  Does the chemical principle you give work in reverse
> like that?
> 
No.

Acid + base gives salt.
Strong acid + salt of weak acid gives weak acid + salt of strong acid.
Strong base + salt of weak base gives weak base + salt of strong base.
Acid + metal gives salt + H2
Base + fat gives soap.

For example, 2HCl+CaCO3 gives CaCl2 + H2CO3 - strong acid + chalk
becomes weak acid + a salt. However, distinguishing between strong and
weak acids or bases is probably too technical for Nethack.

A potion of lye would be an appropriate base, with some punning uses.
Soap can be used to frighten dogs and cats, remove grease, or create
a slippery floor trap. Maybe applying soap to yourself could temporarily
boost charisma, useful for encounters with shopkeepers and succubi.

Dipping candles in a potion of lye turns them into soap. Dipping acid
in lye or vice versa gives two potions of salt water. Dipping corpses
in salt water or acid uses up the potion and resets the corpse's age
to zero. Dipping corroded objects in lye removes one level of
corrosion.

